WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.391
Imagine hearing your own voice come back while you are trying to speak.

2
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.369
In the venue, guests should hear the host and the remote guest.

3
00:00:11.369 --> 00:00:13.881
The call return should carry only the host.

4
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.419
Relate runs inside the browser.

5
00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:21.841
It stores sockets and signal edges.

6
00:00:21.841 --> 00:00:26.282
Cypher traces the original route from each source to its outputs.

7
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.721
No signal loop here.

8
00:00:28.721 --> 00:00:32.690
Channel two sends the remote guest back to the call through aux one.

9
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.452
Patchroom tests possible edits in JavaScript.

10
00:00:38.452 --> 00:00:40.962
Switch off channel two to aux one.

11
00:00:40.962 --> 00:00:42.755
Keep its send to main.

12
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.650
Room mic: “Can you hear me?”

13
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.560
Remote guest: “I hear you.”

14
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.120
Room mic: “Can you hear me?”

15
00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.160
[The remote guest is absent from their own return.]

16
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.560
Remote guest: “I hear you.”

17
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.450
Remote guest: “I hear you.”

18
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.509
Same timeline, two listening points.

19
00:01:08.509 --> 00:01:10.920
The caller no longer hears themselves,

20
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.733
and the venue still hears both voices.

21
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.628
Bring in a patch list as a CSV file.

22
00:01:18.628 --> 00:01:20.681
This stage rig has a feedback loop.

23
00:01:20.681 --> 00:01:21.855
One cable breaks it.

24
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:25.973
Patchroom.

25
00:01:25.973 --> 00:01:30.081
Rehearse your routing before the doors open, right in your browser.

